%% Turning on customer-managed keys for Contoso Files account, and the failure branch when the key is revoked
%% Source: Microsoft Learn - Customer-managed keys for account encryption (key vault requirements, wrapkey unwrapkey get, user-assigned identity at creation, automatic key version update, revocation behaviour and the Azure Files operations that fail); Recover a deleted storage account (restore the vault first); codex/blueprint-azure-files-secure-storage-account.md step 4
sequenceDiagram
  autonumber
  participant OPS as eng-sysops on the netops-v devstation
  participant ARM as Azure Resource Manager
  participant UAMI as User-assigned managed identity id-cmk-stmryfiles01
  participant AKV as Key Vault kv-organization-storage
  participant SA as stmryfiles01 file service
  participant SRV as Contoso File Sync server and SMB clients
  participant MON as Log Analytics and the assessor

  Note over OPS,AKV: Phase 1 - prerequisites that are not optional
  OPS->>AKV: confirm soft delete AND purge protection are enabled
  AKV-->>OPS: both true, otherwise the account update is rejected
  OPS->>AKV: create an RSA or RSA-HSM key, size 2048, 3072 or 4096
  OPS->>UAMI: create the identity first
  Note over OPS,UAMI: A NEW account must use a user-assigned identity. Only an EXISTING account may use a system-assigned one, so the identity is created before the account
  OPS->>AKV: grant the identity get, wrapKey and unwrapKey on the key
  AKV-->>OPS: access policy or RBAC assignment recorded

  Note over OPS,SA: Phase 2 - point the account at the key
  OPS->>ARM: set encryption keySource to Microsoft.Keyvault, name the vault, the key and the identity, and OMIT the key version
  ARM->>SA: apply
  SA->>AKV: wrap the account encryption key with the customer-managed key
  AKV-->>SA: wrapped key returned
  SA-->>ARM: protection is effective immediately, the data itself was never re-encrypted
  Note over SA,AKV: Omitting the version means Azure Storage checks the vault ONCE A DAY for a newer version. After rotating, wait 24 hours before disabling the old version

  Note over SRV,AKV: Phase 3 - steady state
  SRV->>SA: SMB 3.1.1 or FileREST request over the private endpoint
  SA->>AKV: unwrap the account encryption key
  AKV-->>SA: unwrapped
  SA-->>SRV: read or write served

  alt the key is available and the identity still holds get, wrapKey and unwrapKey
    SA-->>MON: normal traffic. Note that a key ROTATION in the vault is not written to the storage resource logs, so the rotation record has to come from the vault side
  else the key is disabled or deleted, or the identity loses its grant
    SRV->>SA: List Directories and Files, Create File, Get File, Get File Properties, Put Range
    SA->>AKV: unwrap
    AKV-->>SA: denied
    SA-->>SRV: 403 Forbidden for ALL users, on exactly that documented list of Azure Files operations
    SRV-->>MON: File Sync 9102 with a non-zero HResult, backup jobs fail, direct mounts stop serving data
    Note over OPS,AKV: This is the intended emergency revocation switch, not a bug. It is also the accident that a vault clean-up causes
    OPS->>AKV: re-enable the key version or restore the grant
    AKV-->>SA: unwrap succeeds again
    SA-->>SRV: service resumes with no data re-encryption and no restore
  end

  Note over OPS,MON: Ordering trap for a disaster - if the storage account AND the vault were both deleted, the vault must be restored BEFORE the account restore is attempted, and account recovery is best-effort within 14 days
  Note over OPS,MON: Contoso decision on record - platform-managed keys meet the baseline. CMK is adopted only if Contoso policy mandates it, because it adds the vault, the identity, the daily version check and this revocation path to the blast radius of every share
